WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.773 --> 00:00:07.190
KAMAU BOBB: My
name is Kamau Bobb.

00:00:07.190 --> 00:00:09.590
I am the global lead
for diversity, strategy,

00:00:09.590 --> 00:00:12.110
and research here at Google.

00:00:12.110 --> 00:00:15.140
The discussion today is
about race, and education,

00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:18.170
and the future of a
modern technopolis.

00:00:18.170 --> 00:00:20.610
In order to get into
that conversation

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:24.780
it's important to understand the
moment that we're currently in.

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:28.410
Obviously the nation here in the
United States and in the world

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:32.159
is in a flux, particularly
the United States

00:00:32.159 --> 00:00:34.890
it's relevant to reflect
a little bit on how we got

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:37.140
to the moment that we're in.

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:41.880
We are in a moment of the
conflation of a COVID pandemic

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:46.590
and a social uprising inspired
by the killing of George Floyd.

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:49.710
But in order to get
into race and education,

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:52.350
we do have to pan
back just a bit

00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:55.110
and think about the
ideological transition

00:00:55.110 --> 00:00:56.580
that the country is in.

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:59.020
However you might feel
about those things,

00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:02.970
the reality is that we've
gone through national trauma.

00:01:02.970 --> 00:01:07.500
In the beginning, at the
shift of these two ideologies,

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:10.760
there was, if you recall,
the Woman's March.

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:14.920
It was the largest demonstration
in American history,

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:16.830
and it was revealing
a certain kind

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:21.060
of angst about the ideology
that we're currently under.

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:22.920
That was followed
almost immediately

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:27.330
by an immigration debacle that
led to uprisings in airports

00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:31.950
all across the country, and
the ban from immigrants coming

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:34.260
from certain Muslim countries.

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:38.490
We were followed that with the
government shutdown, which was,

00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:42.490
again, the longest in the
history of the country.

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:44.970
And then there was
the impeachment,

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:48.210
and all the while
there was the angst

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:53.100
dealing with racial discord
and animus, the Charlottesville

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:57.460
demonstration led to a time
where people who are my age,

00:01:57.460 --> 00:01:59.850
I was born four years after
Martin Luther King was

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:01.980
assassinated, people
who are my age

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:05.070
having to contend with
words like the Ku Klux Klan

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:07.720
and Nazi affiliated
organizations.

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:09.800
For people, again,
in my generation,

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:11.800
those things are
artifacts of history,

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:14.610
but we had to contend
with them in real time.

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:17.550
And then ultimately, here
we are in this COVID-19

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:19.890
global pandemic and
all of the trauma

00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:21.820
that that's put
the country under,

00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:25.110
and in the midst of
that, we had to confront

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:27.000
the killing of
George Floyd, which

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:31.500
has sent us all into what I
would call an American sprint.

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:34.230
So discussing race
and education,

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:37.500
and ultimately what a
modern technopolis is,

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:39.870
it would be incomplete
for us to try

00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:41.680
to do that without
understanding,

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:43.680
at least contemplating
the effects of the moment

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:45.110
that we're in.

00:02:45.110 --> 00:02:50.120
So relative to those two
things, race and education,

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:52.680
the pre-existing conditions
idea is obviously

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:54.300
part of the lexicon
now because we're

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:57.252
talking about a global pandemic
and the disproportionate

00:02:57.252 --> 00:02:58.710
impacts that it
has with people who

00:02:58.710 --> 00:03:01.590
have pre-existing conditions.

00:03:01.590 --> 00:03:03.875
The same is true in
education, and one

00:03:03.875 --> 00:03:06.000
of the principal pre-existing
conditions that we're

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:09.720
dealing with in, at least
United States education,

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:13.530
is segregation, racial
segregation specifically.

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:16.530
And again, here too, we have
to be mindful of the fact

00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:20.910
that we're not talking about
racial segregation in 1954

00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:21.540
previously.

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:24.780
We're talking about it
today, here in 2020.

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:28.680
If you recall, 1954 was Brown
versus Board of Education,

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:31.140
where we decided
as a country that

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:36.000
legalized racial segregation in
schools in America was illegal.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:39.510
Separate is in fact
inherently unequal.

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:41.490
But here we are today.

00:03:41.490 --> 00:03:45.240
I'll use Atlanta where I live
as an example to demonstrate

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:47.610
the degree of racial
segregation that we currently

00:03:47.610 --> 00:03:51.330
operate under, and this is by
no means special to Atlanta.

00:03:51.330 --> 00:03:54.870
Atlanta happens to be one of the
top two most segregated cities

00:03:54.870 --> 00:03:59.280
in the United States, and this
graph crudely, what it shows

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:02.400
is that in the colors that
are dark and gray and black,

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:05.340
that's the predominantly
black section of the city,

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:07.980
and those that are white are
the predominantly white sections

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:09.060
of the city.

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:11.700
And that segregation
is nearly absolute,

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:14.370
and it correlates with
everything, with the quality

00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:17.670
of education, with the
quality of public service,

00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:19.740
and in this current
moment of social upheaval,

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:22.350
it correlates with
the kind and quality

00:04:22.350 --> 00:04:24.600
of interaction with
the police, whether you

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:26.010
get garbage picked up.

00:04:26.010 --> 00:04:28.200
It certainly correlates
with the voting patterns

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:30.710
that we see here in the city.

00:04:30.710 --> 00:04:34.980
But to make it even more
clear, relative to education,

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:38.570
I think it's important for us
to consider how this actually

00:04:38.570 --> 00:04:40.490
plays out, and certainly
for those of us who

00:04:40.490 --> 00:04:43.120
live in the United
States and have children,

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:45.620
you know what this is like in
the schools and communities

00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:48.180
in which you live.

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:51.340
The names here are immaterial,
but these are basically

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:55.750
the 11 traditional public
high schools in Atlanta,

00:04:55.750 --> 00:04:57.310
and all I want to
illustrate here

00:04:57.310 --> 00:05:01.150
is that in essentially
eight of those schools,

00:05:01.150 --> 00:05:03.670
there are virtually no
white students at all.

00:05:03.670 --> 00:05:06.490
And all of the students in the
entire Atlanta Public School

00:05:06.490 --> 00:05:10.120
System, essentially
attend three schools.

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:15.310
So it would be in fact true if
we put a sign here that said,

00:05:15.310 --> 00:05:18.880
these eight schools
are for blacks only.

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:20.590
And hearkening back
to a previous time,

00:05:20.590 --> 00:05:23.500
those kinds of things are
egregious to our sensibilities

00:05:23.500 --> 00:05:27.050
here in the middle
of the 21st century.

00:05:27.050 --> 00:05:28.790
But this is the reality.

00:05:28.790 --> 00:05:32.380
And even in the schools
where white students attend,

00:05:32.380 --> 00:05:34.420
those schools
within them are also

00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:37.990
segregated such that the white
students have better access

00:05:37.990 --> 00:05:40.750
to the advanced placement
courses, to the honors courses,

00:05:40.750 --> 00:05:43.438
to the international
baccalaureate courses,

00:05:43.438 --> 00:05:45.730
and in this instance, I've
been to all of these schools

00:05:45.730 --> 00:05:46.430
personally.

00:05:46.430 --> 00:05:50.020
So-- it's not
secondhand information.

00:05:50.020 --> 00:05:53.680
I know, in fact, in part because
of my role at Georgia Tech,

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:56.600
I understand how this works.

00:05:56.600 --> 00:06:00.760
So this is the street-level
reality of the relationship

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:03.280
between race and education.

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:07.330
Were essentially still living in
two totally bifurcated systems

00:06:07.330 --> 00:06:10.000
with two different
sets of expectations

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:12.520
about what education
means, about what education

00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:15.800
is offered to one group of
students versus the other,

00:06:15.800 --> 00:06:17.860
and clearly that
limits their ability

00:06:17.860 --> 00:06:22.880
to participate when they move
into the post-secondary world.

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:25.540
To demonstrate
that even further,

00:06:25.540 --> 00:06:27.790
at the Georgia Tech
College of Computing,

00:06:27.790 --> 00:06:29.500
I'm using Georgia
Tech as an example

00:06:29.500 --> 00:06:32.055
because I'm on the
faculty there as well.

00:06:32.055 --> 00:06:34.180
I'm the Senior Director of
the Constellation Center

00:06:34.180 --> 00:06:35.800
for Equity and Computing.

00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:39.070
So I'm speaking on behalf
here of information

00:06:39.070 --> 00:06:41.080
that I know well.

00:06:41.080 --> 00:06:44.230
Ultimately what we've
seen over the last decade

00:06:44.230 --> 00:06:49.720
and some is that the fraction
of black and Latinx students

00:06:49.720 --> 00:06:53.050
who have been admitted into
the College of Computing

00:06:53.050 --> 00:06:55.780
has only increased
incrementally,

00:06:55.780 --> 00:06:59.140
but for Asian and white
students, as you can see here,

00:06:59.140 --> 00:07:01.960
there's been a significant
increase, principally

00:07:01.960 --> 00:07:06.380
for Asian students as you can
see that increase is dramatic.

00:07:06.380 --> 00:07:09.350
But what that is
an indication of

00:07:09.350 --> 00:07:12.140
is the basic infrastructure
that is segregated,

00:07:12.140 --> 00:07:14.030
which is the pre-existing
condition that

00:07:14.030 --> 00:07:17.960
limits students' opportunity to
be able to play in this higher

00:07:17.960 --> 00:07:19.040
level.

00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:21.230
And that indicates, of
course, and brings us

00:07:21.230 --> 00:07:25.120
to the idea of what a
modern technopolis is,

00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:29.990
and in my construction of this
term, when cities like Atlanta,

00:07:29.990 --> 00:07:32.720
like Chicago, like
the Bay Area, Oakland,

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:36.140
New York, Raleigh-Durham, and
the Research Triangle Park,

00:07:36.140 --> 00:07:38.930
they have built economic
development strategies

00:07:38.930 --> 00:07:41.660
that are premised on the
idea that tech companies will

00:07:41.660 --> 00:07:47.130
show up in their regions and
drive the economy forward.

00:07:47.130 --> 00:07:50.870
And that is a reasonable
expectation, particularly given

00:07:50.870 --> 00:07:53.510
the disproportionate
positive impacts to economies

00:07:53.510 --> 00:07:56.850
that tech companies have,
but what does that do?

00:07:56.850 --> 00:07:58.970
It requires a certain skill set.

00:07:58.970 --> 00:08:01.520
So when companies,
not unlike Google,

00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:04.820
are planning for where
these locations should be,

00:08:04.820 --> 00:08:06.980
they do an assessment
of the workforce

00:08:06.980 --> 00:08:10.430
and the workforce's
capabilities in those places.

00:08:10.430 --> 00:08:13.130
And in the current,
we're sneaking up

00:08:13.130 --> 00:08:17.150
on the end of the first
quarter of the 21st century,

00:08:17.150 --> 00:08:20.630
and this is news and
language that we all know,

00:08:20.630 --> 00:08:22.710
what are 21st century skills?

00:08:22.710 --> 00:08:26.030
What is the promise of
a modern technopolis?

00:08:26.030 --> 00:08:28.760
The promise is that
we will have people

00:08:28.760 --> 00:08:31.820
who have computational skills,
who have technical skills, who

00:08:31.820 --> 00:08:34.400
have the wherewithal to
be able to participate

00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:36.851
in this modern
economy in some way,

00:08:36.851 --> 00:08:38.809
and that's a range from
people who are software

00:08:38.809 --> 00:08:41.530
engineers and advanced
computation lists,

00:08:41.530 --> 00:08:45.230
to those who can use basic
analytical and numerical skills

00:08:45.230 --> 00:08:47.460
to participate in
whatever industry,

00:08:47.460 --> 00:08:50.950
whether it be art, cultural
generation, service industries,

00:08:50.950 --> 00:08:52.970
gig-economies, et cetera.

00:08:52.970 --> 00:08:55.130
So we have to
agree that there is

00:08:55.130 --> 00:08:58.640
some certain level of
numeracy and analytical skills

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:01.070
and technical skills
that make people

00:09:01.070 --> 00:09:06.360
able to participate in what
I call a modern technopolis.

00:09:06.360 --> 00:09:08.790
So the challenge
here, of course,

00:09:08.790 --> 00:09:12.530
is that if we're thinking
about what citizenship means

00:09:12.530 --> 00:09:16.370
in that space, and here
I don't mean citizenship

00:09:16.370 --> 00:09:20.240
in the litigious sense of who
is legally able to qualify

00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:23.900
to be a citizen, but
rather citizenship

00:09:23.900 --> 00:09:26.390
in the sense of
mutual responsibility

00:09:26.390 --> 00:09:31.220
and the ability to be viable,
to be meaningful, to be present,

00:09:31.220 --> 00:09:33.860
and to have a means
of contributing

00:09:33.860 --> 00:09:38.320
in this modern technopolis,
in this modern economy.

00:09:38.320 --> 00:09:42.620
In order to do that, you have to
have the aforementioned skills.

00:09:42.620 --> 00:09:47.440
But because the segregation is
so virulent, and what it does

00:09:47.440 --> 00:09:50.590
is it constrains the
opportunity of certain sections

00:09:50.590 --> 00:09:53.680
of these cities to be
able to participate.

00:09:53.680 --> 00:09:55.810
In the case of Atlanta,
what I was alluding to,

00:09:55.810 --> 00:09:59.440
to make this case ultimately,
is that we already

00:09:59.440 --> 00:10:02.380
know that black students
in the public school

00:10:02.380 --> 00:10:05.320
system in Atlanta,
for example, are

00:10:05.320 --> 00:10:08.440
segregated in ways
that lock them out

00:10:08.440 --> 00:10:12.520
of their ability to participate
to get the access to higher

00:10:12.520 --> 00:10:14.620
Ed. institutions
like Georgia Tech,

00:10:14.620 --> 00:10:16.547
like Stanford, like
Carnegie Mellon,

00:10:16.547 --> 00:10:18.880
like the current competitive
and selective institutions,

00:10:18.880 --> 00:10:22.420
and those of course, are not the
only higher Ed. institutions.

00:10:22.420 --> 00:10:25.960
But when companies are planning
where they ought to go,

00:10:25.960 --> 00:10:29.650
particularly tech companies,
they use those institutions

00:10:29.650 --> 00:10:33.100
as proxy for their availability
of a modern workforce.

00:10:33.100 --> 00:10:35.620
So if we already
know our priority

00:10:35.620 --> 00:10:39.160
that the system that we
have completely and totally

00:10:39.160 --> 00:10:42.700
and structurally locks
out certain sections

00:10:42.700 --> 00:10:46.450
of the population,
and at the same time,

00:10:46.450 --> 00:10:49.720
we build an economic
development strategy that's

00:10:49.720 --> 00:10:53.020
premised on the fact that we
know that those students can't

00:10:53.020 --> 00:10:55.900
compete, we're
building a future,

00:10:55.900 --> 00:10:57.970
we're building an
idea of citizenship

00:10:57.970 --> 00:11:00.490
in a modern technopolis
without them.

00:11:00.490 --> 00:11:04.930
So that means the mass
migration out of technopolises,

00:11:04.930 --> 00:11:09.580
of technopoli if you like,
without the black population,

00:11:09.580 --> 00:11:12.310
the Latinx population, the
poor population in rural

00:11:12.310 --> 00:11:14.590
communities, where
those students,

00:11:14.590 --> 00:11:16.615
because of the structure
in which they sit

00:11:16.615 --> 00:11:20.710
in their educational
enclaves, cannot participate.

00:11:20.710 --> 00:11:24.610
And when we plan for and
build an infrastructure

00:11:24.610 --> 00:11:28.690
to generate economic development
in a technopolis, knowing that,

00:11:28.690 --> 00:11:31.420
we're planning for a
future without them.

00:11:31.420 --> 00:11:33.430
We have to be clear
about what that means,

00:11:33.430 --> 00:11:35.630
and in an environment
that we're currently in,

00:11:35.630 --> 00:11:38.530
which is why I began
this discussion--

00:11:38.530 --> 00:11:41.270
in the way that I did, because
we're in a social uprising

00:11:41.270 --> 00:11:42.120
here.

00:11:42.120 --> 00:11:43.720
We're contending
with what it means

00:11:43.720 --> 00:11:46.330
to be an American
citizen, to be viable,

00:11:46.330 --> 00:11:49.150
to have equal and human rights,
to have your presence be

00:11:49.150 --> 00:11:52.120
acknowledged, be
respected, and cherished,

00:11:52.120 --> 00:11:56.050
and if in this specific line
of dealing with technopoli,

00:11:56.050 --> 00:11:59.440
we're planning for futures
without certain people,

00:11:59.440 --> 00:12:03.563
it conflates this other problem
that we're contending with now.

00:12:03.563 --> 00:12:05.480
So as we go forward, our
challenges are clear.

00:12:05.480 --> 00:12:07.588
These are things that
all of us are contending

00:12:07.588 --> 00:12:09.380
with in the individual
decisions that we're

00:12:09.380 --> 00:12:11.213
making with our schools,
with our companies,

00:12:11.213 --> 00:12:14.550
with our non-profit
organizations, et cetera.

00:12:14.550 --> 00:12:17.430
But access resources
and philosophy.

00:12:17.430 --> 00:12:19.860
Access obviously is to
the kind of education

00:12:19.860 --> 00:12:21.540
that makes you viable.

00:12:21.540 --> 00:12:23.220
The resources is
the infrastructure

00:12:23.220 --> 00:12:27.660
that makes those educational
enclaves palatable, functional,

00:12:27.660 --> 00:12:30.120
to make sure that
teachers are prepared.

00:12:30.120 --> 00:12:31.780
If you recall in
the last two years,

00:12:31.780 --> 00:12:34.197
there are about 11 states where
the public school teachers

00:12:34.197 --> 00:12:36.240
across the country
were on strike because

00:12:36.240 --> 00:12:37.980
of inadequate resources.

00:12:37.980 --> 00:12:40.120
And what does that
all aggregate to?

00:12:40.120 --> 00:12:41.160
A philosophy.

00:12:41.160 --> 00:12:45.570
What is our philosophy about
the utility of education

00:12:45.570 --> 00:12:47.100
in a Democratic-Republic?

00:12:47.100 --> 00:12:50.430
To make sure that all of the
students have the wherewithal,

00:12:50.430 --> 00:12:53.970
there are all in an
integrated part of what

00:12:53.970 --> 00:12:56.640
a modern technopolis is.

00:12:56.640 --> 00:12:59.670
So I would charge
us, charge myself

00:12:59.670 --> 00:13:03.360
with trying to consider
what we do, and I think

00:13:03.360 --> 00:13:09.860
where we go from here, because
the country is in such a flux,

00:13:09.860 --> 00:13:14.360
our bias is towards
making a quick act to try

00:13:14.360 --> 00:13:17.300
to bolster deficits
that we can easily see.

00:13:17.300 --> 00:13:19.160
There aren't enough devices.

00:13:19.160 --> 00:13:21.470
There aren't enough
students in communities

00:13:21.470 --> 00:13:23.210
with broadband connectivity.

00:13:23.210 --> 00:13:25.330
All of those things
are important,

00:13:25.330 --> 00:13:28.540
but my argument is
in the consideration

00:13:28.540 --> 00:13:31.300
of race and education,
what we have to do here

00:13:31.300 --> 00:13:34.630
is we have to contend
with our philosophy.

00:13:34.630 --> 00:13:38.860
We're asking the public
education infrastructure

00:13:38.860 --> 00:13:40.930
in the United
States, which I would

00:13:40.930 --> 00:13:45.790
argue is one of the pillars of
our Democratic-Republic, what

00:13:45.790 --> 00:13:48.940
is it that we are choosing
to invest in that such

00:13:48.940 --> 00:13:52.090
that we don't a priori
decide that there are

00:13:52.090 --> 00:13:55.630
certain sets of students who
have essentially no future,

00:13:55.630 --> 00:13:57.460
irrespective of
their own abilities,

00:13:57.460 --> 00:14:00.190
ambitions, and capabilities,
they don't have

00:14:00.190 --> 00:14:03.100
a future in this country
that we're trying to build,

00:14:03.100 --> 00:14:07.790
particularly one that is
driven by a technopolis idea.

00:14:07.790 --> 00:14:10.600
So those are the charges that
I think that we're up against.

00:14:10.600 --> 00:14:12.190
It's a moment for
us all to consider

00:14:12.190 --> 00:14:15.760
the very best of the
original American principles

00:14:15.760 --> 00:14:19.410
and a global sense of
what it means to be human.

00:14:19.410 --> 00:14:21.170
Thank you much.

